.\" $OpenBSD$
.\" Public domain.
.Dd $Mdocdate: September 6 2026 $
.Dt BTRFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm btrfs
.Nd inspect and administer Btrfs filesystems
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm subvolume list
.Ar mountpoint
.Nm
.Cm subvolume create
.Ar mountpoint path
.Nm
.Cm subvolume delete
.Ar mountpoint path
.Nm
.Cm subvolume snapshot
.Op Fl r
.Ar mountpoint source path
.Nm
.Cm inspect-internal
.Ar command ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility inspects Btrfs filesystems and administers their subvolumes.
Use
.Fl Fl help
after a command to display its usage.
.Pp
For subvolume commands,
.Ar mountpoint
selects a mounted Btrfs filesystem.
All source and destination paths start at the top-level subvolume (ID 5),
regardless of which subvolume is mounted there.
A leading slash is optional.
Parent components
.Pq Sq ..
and symbolic links are not followed.
The destination parent directory must already exist.
These commands require superuser privileges and use
.Pa /dev/btrfs-control .
.Bl -tag -width "subvolume snapshot"
.It Cm subvolume list
List all linked subvolumes in the filesystem, including their IDs,
parent subvolume IDs, read-only state, and filesystem-relative paths.
The top-level subvolume is implicit and is not listed.
.It Cm subvolume create
Create an empty writable subvolume.
.It Cm subvolume delete
Remove the named subvolume and its contents.
A subvolume with nested subvolumes must have those removed first.
Mounted subvolumes and subvolumes with active vnodes cannot be deleted.
.It Cm subvolume snapshot
Create a writable snapshot of the exact source subvolume.
The
.Fl r
option creates a read-only snapshot.
Snapshots share metadata and file-data extents with their source;
later modifications use copy-on-write.
.El
.Pp
For example, if subvolume
.Pa home
is mounted at
.Pa /home ,
the following creates its sibling snapshot without mounting ID 5:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
btrfs subvolume snapshot -r /home home home@monday
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/btrfs-control"
.It Pa /dev/btrfs-control
Filesystem administration device.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount_btrfs 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Subvolume deletion currently reserves metadata for the complete operation.
It may fail for lack of metadata space even when deleting the subvolume
would eventually free enough space.
Listing is ordered by subvolume ID; concurrent commands can change the
list between calls.
